  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186115299,
      "id": "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac",
      "title": "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "762f1d9f-287f-5bfe-9b9a-69ca89615084",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+2z &=-4 \\\\ -2x-3y-6z &=9 \\\\ -x-4y+z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent independent\n- **II.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **III.** The system is inconsistent",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(2){R}_{1} \\\\ & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(1){R}_{1} \\\\ & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(2){R}_{2} \\\\ & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is a pivot for each variable and no contradictory row. The system has a unique solution, so we conclude that the system is consistent independent.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "3232d831-cf6d-59fd-94e4-698f41fcc7f2",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
